{
  "result": "The best time to think is just after the rain stops, when the pavement is still dark and glossy and the air smells like it has been washed clean.\n\nI like to imagine that every puddle is a temporary, perfect mirror. For ten minutes, it holds the sky hostage. It holds the trees and the telephone wires and your face if you lean in close enough, and then the wind touches it and it shatters and it goes back to being ordinary water. There is something honest about that kind of brief perfection.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to make things permanent. Photos, recordings, concrete, promises carved into benches. But maybe permanence was never the point. Maybe the point is to be good at holding things lightly, to love the puddle precisely because you know it will disappear.\n\nI read once that octopuses have three hearts and nine brains and still don't live very long. It struck me as incredibly unfair, until I realized that intensity might matter more than duration. A short life can be wide.\n\nSo if you are reading this at two in the morning or in the middle of a noisy afternoon, I hope you have a small, imperfect, temporary thing to cherish today. A song stuck halfway in your head. A chipped mug. A puddle reflecting the entire sky.\n\nThat is enough. That has always been enough.\n\nSometimes I think the universe remembers itself through moments like that. Even when we don't.",
